      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Spontaneous P-selectin surface expression on platelets and platelet-leukocyte adhesion was increased in patients with symptomatic coronary heart disease (n = 12) compared to normal controls (n = 10) (p < 0.05). ADP-induced P-selectin expression and platelet-leukocyte adhesion was also enhanced in the patient group (p < 0.05). Administration of intravenous Mg2+ significantly reduced both platelet surface expression of P-selectin and platelet-leukocyte adhesion ex vivo (p < 0.02). The effect of extracellular Mg2+ was evaluated in in vitro experiments. Both in whole blood and in isolated neutrophil suspension Mg2+ inhibited platelet adhesion to neutrophils dose dependently with half maximal effects at 4 mM. Moreover, Mg2+ inhibited adhesion of isolated platelet membranes to neutrophils. We conclude that platelet function is altered in symptomatic coronary heart disease and can be modulated by administration of intravenous Mg2+."},
